REM {"id": "M2-c417", "lesson": "L08", "front": "Theorem 1.2 of Lesson 8: what does Rolle's theorem state there, and how does it relate to the mean value theorem?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 1.1, let p < q. If phi is continuous on [p, q], differentiable on (p, q), and phi(p) = phi(q), then phi'(c) = 0 for some c with p < c < q. It is the special case of the mean value theorem (Theorem 2.3 of Lesson 6) with equal endpoint values, and Lesson 8 uses it in no other form (Remark 1.3).", "grade": "T"}
REM {"id": "M2-c428", "lesson": "L08", "front": "What is Assumption 1.1 in Lesson 8?", "back": "The extreme value theorem, assumed and not proved: a function continuous on a closed interval [p, q] with p < q attains a maximum value and a minimum value on [p, q]. It is Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6, and no grade is given to it."}
REM {"id": "M2-c429", "lesson": "L10", "grade": "T", "front": "Theorem 1.2 of Lesson 10 (mean value theorem): what does it state?", "back": "Assuming Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6, let p < q. If phi is continuous on [p, q] and differentiable on (p, q), then there is c with p < c < q and phi(q) - phi(p) = phi'(c)(q - p). It is Theorem 2.3 of Lesson 6, used here without reproof (Remark 1.3)."}
